WEBVTT

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:05.550
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:33.330
 What OPENrestraunt was doing was creating these large-scale immersive events that were like environments where you could walk into an art space and like a swing with a kid would pass you by and then you would hear chickens fluttering underneath the table because they were running around the whole art space as people were sitting down to eat dinner and someone was running a soil tasting bar.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:51.680
 And, you know, you're invited really by the way the food was arrived on your plate to think about where it came from and what that tasted like and for me that shared sensory experience was so exciting.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:02.560
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Chelsea Wills and me, Devon Samson.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:10.340
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.340
 and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:21.410
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food and who have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:29.940
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app, and you can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:38.960
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:47.400
 I'm Chelsea Wills, and on September 30th, I interviewed artist and friend Amanda Eicher at Southern Exposure in San Francisco.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.840
 Amanda's work investigates the ways groups engage in creative thinking.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:02.520
 Her practice looks at the intersections between traditional community-based art practices and contemporary approaches to social engagement in art.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:11.870
 With open restaurants, she has shown at SF MoMA, the Berkeley Art Museum, the 14th Venice Architecture Biennial, and many other places.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.540
 She is now doing residency at the DeRosa Preserve.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.840
 Let's start by talking about some projects you've done about food over the years.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:28.600
 I dug my first garden when I was in high school.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:38.280
 I started working when I was 14 in a bakery at a farmer's market, and over time I thought,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:46.160
 I think I understand food in a way that's fun for me and interesting.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:54.510
 I think the pressure to earn money at a pretty early age made me really look for opportunities.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:03:05.290
 But I started catering business lunches for small businesses around town, really in a crockpot,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.270
 making something that was easy to transport and eat.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:17.860
 And as I was doing that, the tiny amount of business acumen that I have or had at the time was like,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.190
 you could do this a lot better if you had a garden.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.980
 And so I really set about trying to grow as much as I could.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:36.770
 And I think that early experience really solidified this thing in my mind that maybe we work against a little bit now,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:43.240
 which is that gardening could be a way to ease financial stresses.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.450
 And I'm sort of a picture book case of how that did work.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:54.210
 But I was a kid making money to kind of help our household work things out.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:04:06.810
 I wasn't, say, a working parent of two kids like my mom or, you know, someone with more heavy financial responsibility.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:14.850
 At the same time, I think it provided the platform for a great link between food and the way we live our lives.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:28.060
 And I always, through high school, through college, I just kind of had an eye on what was happening at Chez Panisse and the Animal Schoolyard Foundation.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:35.150
 And that phrase, a garden in every classroom, is a very impactful one.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.610
 And I can say, as someone growing up across the country in the East Coast, it made sense to me.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:54.690
 And so when I came to California, I pretty soon after I arrived, got an artist's grant from the California Arts Council to work with a preschool,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:59.230
 creating a schoolyard garden around the cultures represented in that school.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:09.060
 And, you know, it kind of took shape from there, on and off, working with communities.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:19.940
 And I think in 2003, beginning to travel to El Salvador, I was working with agricultural communities so that whatever conversation we had,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:34.270
 whether it was about oral history or performance or body movement, sports, you know, it all tied back to agriculture and land.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:45.930
 And, you know, at the end of the project period, after we had made 10 murals in town, someone would stand up and just say,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:54.070
 you know, the really important thing is that we remember to take care of our environment.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:06:06.540
 You know, I remember the year I stopped eating purslane because it came from a field that was infested in pesticides and other chemicals.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:17.450
 And, you know, that person who was maybe 60 or 70 would just kind of testify in front of the group about how sweet that purslane salad was

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.840
 and how much they missed that perfect lunch on a day of work.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:30.510
 And I think I just, my ears really tune to those conversations, you know, where we bring it back to something real,

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:36.300
 something daily and something that we all do in our own way.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:50.540
 I guess I haven't really talked about such a wide range of projects, but maybe that sets up why I would be drawn toward a project like Open Restaurant.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:56.860
 Well, I'm so glad you started there instead of actually talking about what projects you've done about food,

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.350
 because it's, those are the stories about food, right?

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:08.870
 They're nestled in our lives in these ways that are really complicated and really tangled in like the fabric of who we are.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:20.330
 So if you could, maybe just, you did this project in El Salvador for a long time and I didn't really realize that that had so much to do with agriculture there.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:25.760
 So it had a lot to do with food and really explicit and really implicit ways.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.190
 You've worked with gardens for a long time and it sounds like lots of different capacities.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:32.990
 Is that true?

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.420
 Not as much as some, but more than others, I think.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:44.980
 I'm not, by no means a specialist, but, um, but it is really important to me.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:53.260
 And, um, like I realized the way that some of these things are really embedded in my daily life.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:08:01.600
 Like, you know, I've been gardening all these years and still this year, I was like, now that's so interesting.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:08.050
 Every time I bend over to pull weeds, I think of someone that I need to write a thank you note to.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:19.010
 There's like a really thankful bodily moment that happens when I'm like doubled over with my face really close to the ground.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:43.570
 And I, I think it's just, you know, in terms of gardening, it's interesting to notice, um, that there are still new things to learn and that they can be as basic as these kind of body postures or ways that this square foot of earth, you know, to kind of like call out Wendell Derry can relate to the rest of the world and how we live in it.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.670
 I think that's so important.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:09:16.680
 And how is the trajectory from having your face near the earth and weeding and growing food to, well, I think in a more public way, it's always tapped back and forth between, um, these concerns about how communities grow and develop and, um, what we're all doing in relation to the food systems or the agricultural systems we find ourselves living with.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:35.530
 Um, but, um, I was, I have been working with, um, this agricultural cooperative town in El Salvador for about 10 years, um, when I went back to grad school and I had also had some experience in Rwanda.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:52.100
 So, um, um, CEC ArtsLink, which is sort of really amazing organization, um, asked me to come on a newish initiative that they had to bring artists into, um, locations along the Silk Road.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:01.720
 Um, I was chosen by a contemporary art center in Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan to come and work with children who are semi-nomadic.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:20.580
 Um, so this, you know, in, in most ways has nothing to do with food, um, but it does have to do with how these families navigate systems and, um, think about themselves, um, in relation to this space, which was a contemporary art space.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:31.580
 And, um, I think in a bigger way, um, all of those projects were about understanding, um, this very extended marketplace of the Silk Road.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:47.240
 Um, and, um, I did do a project in Bishkek with, with kids, but I found myself also traveling quite a bit to the other project sites, especially Osh, where, um, open restaurants running a two-week restaurant in a market.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:55.170
 And it was so exciting to be part of this conversation about how do we situate them?

00:10:55.610 --> 00:11:02.370
 How do we situate ourselves as outsiders and invite people to really make themselves at home in this space?

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:11.570
 And I think, um, you know, I say we, because it also felt so easy to become part of the conversation.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:26.150
 Um, and I think that's something about open restaurant, you know, the openness is trying to create a place where people with ideas and energy and curiosity feel invited.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:41.440
 And just like you do when you go to someone's house for dinner, you feel invited to sit down and start kind of engaging with the material that's in front of you, whether that means eating it or planting it or rearranging it or, um, taking it apart.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:12:05.360
 Um, we, I really found it so lovely to be in that marketplace in Osh, thinking about how this lunch counter could also be a place where university students could, um, do design work and where grandmothers could bring their traditional Korean carrots salad and explain it to us.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:10.700
 And, um, and, um, and where workers could get a meal for about the same prices at any other stand.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:18.100
 And, um, when we came back, I just, I continued wanting to work with open restaurant.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:25.810
 I felt really curious about the big side of what they did, even though that was a big project.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:35.370
 Um, I, I thought about the projects that were aimed at large audiences of like a thousand people or more.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:37.290
 And I felt like I didn't know how to do that.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:38.770
 And I wanted to learn.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.190
 So I think that that was kind of my introduction.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.840
 So who has been involved with open risk?

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.660
 What is open restaurant, I guess, and who has been involved?

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.320
 And yeah, that's the beginning of the story.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:00.070
 So how did you actually, what was the first project and what did that look like?

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:08.120
 Well, open restaurant is a collective of chefs and artists, um, who take the traditional architectures

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:17.130
 and, um, rules or ways of interaction, interacting of the restaurant and move it into an art space

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:26.030
 as a way to, um, take it apart, ask questions, re, reconstruct what it might be able to be.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.210
 And, um, um, or what we could do there.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.420
 And, um, I think especially we wanted to ask questions about the food system in this really broad way,

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:43.370
 our water system, the land we live on, um, education and, um, social politics,

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:47.150
 all is translated through the meal you find on your plate.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:57.180
 Um, and I think, um, you know, it's important that at the beginning it was Sam White and Jerome

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:07.910
 Vogt, um, Stacey Pierce, and then Sasha Wazanski, um, many, many, many, many, many others even

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:17.130
 before I came, but Valerie Imus and I took a strong role in 2011 or 10, helping to develop

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.940
 a project called Open Water.

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:27.210
 Um, and what Open Restaurant was doing when they weren't in Kyrgyzstan or off outside of California

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:33.900
 was, um, really creating these large scale immersive events that were like environments

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:41.970
 where, um, rather than reading about something or asking someone or, you know, listening to

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:52.560
 a podcast, um, you could walk into an art space and, and really like a swing with a kid

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:56.720
 would pass you by and then you would hear chickens fluttering underneath the table because they

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:58.620
 were running around the whole art space.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:05.690
 As people were sitting down to eat dinner and, um, someone was running a soil tasting bar and,

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:15.550
 um, there was a farmer giving a class on how to calibrate topsoil and, um, you know, you're

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:25.250
 invited really by the way the food was arrived on your plate to think about where it came from

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.630
 and what that tasted like.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:32.780
 And, and for me that shared sensory experience was so exciting.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:40.780
 It was so exciting to think that, you know, by constructing this artwork, people could intuit

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:46.770
 through taste or read through taste or, um, somehow taste these phenomena that were kind

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:50.760
 of too big to understand through our minds alone.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.800
 So, um, so that felt really fun.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:16:03.840
 Can you talk a little bit more about, about why that's, that felt important or why that

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:06.120
 maybe still feels interesting?

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:17.020
 Um, well, I think to jump forward really far, um, in 2015 and 2016, we're doing a project at

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:23.520
 the DeRosa, um, looking at how we might be able to taste climate change.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:35.420
 And, you know, here in California, that's such a, um, loud idea that things are changing and

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:46.740
 that we can feel it in the severity of the fires that we experience in the kind of depths of

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:55.330
 the drought in the, you know, almost every weather phenomenon seems tied to this idea that the

00:16:55.330 --> 00:17:01.380
 climate is changing and, you know, we're a waterfront town, San Francisco and the Bay area.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:05.430
 And a lot of what we see will be really different in 50 years.

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:14.740
 Um, so we, I think it's very much on everyone's minds, but it's a little hard to get a grasp.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:23.090
 Uh, and so as a way to be able to understand this ourselves, we thought it would be nice to

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:33.410
 be able to sit down to a meal where the wine that's poured really has required that winemakers

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:38.850
 bring out the flavors that they normally correct for as the climate changes.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:45.120
 You know, here, the vines are flowering much earlier than they normally would.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:51.560
 The fruit comes earlier, um, it ripens very quickly and the flavors are different.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:18:03.210
 And so, um, you know, everything is being rebalanced and winemakers have a huge array of techniques

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:08.100
 to be able to create the best wine out of that situation.

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:14.900
 But what would it be like if we really allowed ourselves to taste the fastest ripening grape

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:26.790
 or the, um, or the kind of over ripeness that comes from picking a grape at the time you normally

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:32.580
 would, and it's been on the vine a little too long, or, um, we had a lot of questions like

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:32.900
 that.

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:39.890
 And so over the next couple of years, we'll be developing, um, sort of a kit or a sculpture

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:50.860
 that, um, you can take apart onto your table and, um, taste what happens when, um, our, the

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:56.420
 pancetta comes from pigs that have eaten really dry forage.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:19:06.080
 And, um, what happens to our bees when, um, the water is scarcer, maybe saltier in California,

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:13.320
 um, you know, um, you know, it really tastes what's changing and, um, bring together groups

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:24.010
 of people from, you know, grape pickers to winemakers to, um, construction workers to farmers

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:28.660
 to specialists to really sit down around the sink table and talk about it.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:36.380
 Why don't, that sounds like a really interesting project and it's changed a lot since the last

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:37.520
 time we talked about it.

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:38.520
 So that's fun to hear.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:43.660
 Um, could you describe one of the projects you've already done in kind of a more tangible

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:44.040
 way?

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:52.380
 So, um, people have a sense of maybe the scope of what Open Restaurant has done, or maybe you

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:59.870
 can kind of touch on a few in terms of what it's felt like to be at those well experiences.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:03.940
 No, I really have to say there have only been a couple that I have.

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:11.070
 Participated in, um, Open Restaurant has done projects at New Lengthen Arts and Yerba Buena

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:19.400
 Center for the Arts and SFMOMA, um, and all of those have been spectacular and, um, overwhelming

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:23.040
 in, in really effective ways.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:32.860
 Um, but for me, I was present in 2010, I think it was for, um, Open Water, which was a very

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:33.880
 large scale event.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:40.050
 And I think that in the end, 3,000 people attended and similarly, um, Open Education in

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.710
 2011 had a similar size crowd.

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:51.090
 So, um, both were very big events and, um, the idea was to create access in every way

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:51.690
 that we could.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:57.880
 And certainly, I think especially in Open Water, we weren't 100% successful.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:05.430
 Um, but, you know, when you walked into Open Water, first you, you needed to go to Alameda,

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:14.550
 which is an island, and, um, find your way to, uh, an enormous old airplane hangar that's now

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:19.480
 the home of St. George Spirits and, um, at the time was also Hangar One Vodka.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:26.610
 So, really big place and, and a production place, um, and, and pretty high end.

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:32.380
 Um, and in that space, you saw a complete water system.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:38.960
 So, there was a block of ice that was, I think, I, I don't know, I'm probably exaggerating to

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:44.440
 say it was half a ton, but it was a really big block of ice melting into a copper still

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:51.780
 that then ran the water through to the dishwasher and the, um, and the restaurant sink that we

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:59.900
 had set up inside this warehouse because it was turned into a working restaurant, um, with

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:06.510
 the modification that the dishwashers were in the middle of the restaurant and the cooks

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:07.890
 were pushed to the side.

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:17.850
 Um, the water system ended in, uh, gray water treatment phyto-remediation zone, which was

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:21.790
 a part of the seating area rather than kind of pushed away to the back.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:24.610
 So, we really wanted to turn things inside out.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:32.480
 And, um, I think the spectacular part, you know, it was a 60-table restaurant and the meals

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:37.900
 we served were very complex, um, because we were trying to tell a story in every meal.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:42.970
 But, you know, you didn't have to sit down at the table to experience that story either

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:51.190
 because there was a video program projected quite large, um, there were performances throughout

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:58.470
 the evenings when we were serving dinner and, um, it turned into a classroom during the day.

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:07.060
 We had students from Contra Costa College come and take classes on, um, the kinds of fish and

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:14.030
 the biology of, um, San Francisco's Bay, um, the Exploratorium sent over one of their exhibit

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:21.520
 designers to create this incredible lighting system made out of plankton, um, that you could

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:22.420
 play with.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.580
 I mean, you have to give them air, so you had to pump air into the plankton.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:35.910
 And, um, we had artists really shaping every part of this temporary restaurant, which existed for

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:36.670
 three days.

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:46.470
 And I think for us, it was important that part of that, um, part of the aesthetics was the

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:47.050
 conversation.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:56.130
 And so, you know, the almost as big as the restaurant table seating area was the area called the tank,

00:23:56.130 --> 00:24:04.480
 which had, um, you know, seven hours of programming every day, including, um, playing poker with

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:11.010
 fishermen and hearing from reporters on the state of California's water battles.

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:20.610
 And, um, we had, um, we had an artist from Humboldt County come and give a talk on the dams and the

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:31.730
 salmon, um, stock, kind of trying to keep a foothold in the increasingly managed water system in

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:32.410
 Humboldt County.

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:38.520
 Um, we had a pickle class and, and a class on salting your food.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:46.460
 And we also had, um, David Wilson and some other artists giving sailboat tours around the

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:46.800
 island.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:56.140
 So, um, we tried to take in this water system that we find ourselves in, in every way that

00:24:56.140 --> 00:24:56.780
 we could.

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:06.230
 Um, you know, there were, there were water tasting, there was a water tasting bar with water from

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:07.790
 places all over California.

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:17.730
 Um, but, uh, it was an effort to make our research public.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:24.080
 And I think in that situation, it was incredibly hard to get to.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:29.220
 It's not that easy for a lot of people to get to the SF MoMA, no less Almeida.

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:33.770
 Um, and it was expensive to eat dinner.

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:39.390
 I don't know that, you know, if you come to Almeida, you might be hungry.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:45.630
 And the small plates we had were not that filling for someone who doesn't have $60 to lay down

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:49.000
 for a grass fed, uh, crepinette.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:57.100
 So, um, there, there were a lot of difficulties in that project for me, I think coming from where

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:57.720
 I come from.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:06.830
 But I think that led us to a project that was very open and was very accessible and, and

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:16.440
 was meant in a way expressly to feed people for free, which was, um, open education, which

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:23.220
 was a, um, celebration of 40 years of, um, the food movement, beginning with the establishment

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:27.520
 of Chez Panisse in Berkeley and 40 years of the free speech movement.

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:36.410
 And considering how factory farming has grown up with, um, a certain factory form of education

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:45.130
 and how, um, how alternatives have really grown up and thrived in Berkeley.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:54.460
 So, you know, for that two day event, we, we created a kitchen in 14 stations throughout

00:26:54.460 --> 00:27:04.760
 the garden of the UC Berkeley art museum and, you know, somehow, even though practically none

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:09.890
 of it was really allowed, we had a police car where people stood on the roof of the police

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:10.930
 car and gave talks.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:21.140
 And we had a radio station built and, and DJed by, um, like nine to 12 year olds broadcasting

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:24.920
 throughout the, the art museum and its yard.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:30.760
 And we had a herd of goats and, uh, and an airstream trailer full of bees.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:36.740
 And we had Chris Sollers and, um, members of the San Francisco Mime Troop baking digger

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:37.180
 bread.

00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:47.960
 And, um, we asked Al from Al's attire in North Beach to make us a new pair of Werner Herzog's

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:49.260
 desert boots.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:56.400
 And we cooked them in a vegetable soup created by Waldorf school children who were teaching people

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.400
 life skills.

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:04.740
 Um, and we served it to Les Blank and Tom Letty and, and a whole bunch of other people

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:05.300
 right there.

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:19.670
 And so I think in all of those 14 small, uh, situations, we were able to really tap into

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:27.280
 this history that was really important to us and also create something that, um, had this

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:34.320
 flavor that you could live in for an hour or a day and that kept on presenting interesting

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:34.980
 questions.

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:43.940
 Like, um, again, students from Metas, which is, uh, an achievement program for students in

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:47.950
 the Latino and low income communities around Contra Costa College.

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:55.690
 Um, we had them come and participate again because I work really closely with them and these young

00:28:55.690 --> 00:29:04.090
 scientists were mixing alternative soft drinks for the crowd using, um, elixirs and other things

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:07.820
 that an artist named Alison Pebworth, um, prepared for them.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:15.500
 But, you know, it was, it was really amazing to see these young chemists and physicists looking

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:25.320
 at, um, this formula with their own kind of analytic framework and, um, working with an artist to make

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:33.690
 these really delicious flavors and, you know, standing there drinking them, you ask, what is Coke?

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:35.610
 Where does it come from?

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:39.680
 What, what is sugar really doing in this world?

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:47.830
 And, and how do, how does that relate to the place where, um, these kids come from, to all

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:54.340
 the places where sugar comes from for us in the U S, um, how has that become corn syrup?

00:29:54.340 --> 00:30:02.880
 And, um, where, you know, is there a lab on UC Berkeley's campus where we can find the answers

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:10.160
 to these things, but, um, just asking questions that might lead to profitable conversations, like

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:17.260
 really effervescent, um, exchange between people who, who really do know about these things.

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:26.690
 Um, so, you know, I think one thing that might be important to point out is that there's no

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:31.950
 way that we could know about all of this stuff ourselves, even though, you know, open restaurant

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:36.370
 is maybe five, six, seven people planning these events.

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:43.760
 Um, it grows to a hundred people in order to do them, but there even a hundred people can't

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:51.530
 know the answers to all these questions, but, um, taking in all the experts that we engage

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:59.970
 with and, um, really presenting them in a light that makes it possible for people to like climb

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:07.990
 up right next to the beehive and talk to a beekeeper, um, or ride a bicycle powered wheat grinder

00:31:07.990 --> 00:31:11.590
 and talk about, um, edible education.

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:16.690
 I think, I think that changes things for the person doing that.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:22.060
 And it could be the person who's never done that before, but I think it also works for the

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:24.970
 person who's done it, you know, maybe once or twice already.

00:31:25.790 --> 00:31:35.430
 I think that brings up for me, like, um, something about holding the space for this as art.

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:44.320
 Um, like what is that and what are the, I guess, what are the boundaries or what are kind of

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:48.440
 the borderlands of what that feels like in this kind of work?

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:55.610
 And then also what are the possibilities that are either deep at the center of it or exist

00:31:55.610 --> 00:31:59.430
 maybe by accident in the process of these things?

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:04.950
 Well, I think, you know, this is my very personal opinion.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:11.090
 I don't know what everyone else in open restaurant would say, but, you know, to me on a certain

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:18.420
 level, um, there's this thing they say when you're training a dog, dogs do what work, what

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:18.880
 works.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:26.840
 And I feel like it's art because it works, you know, it, it manages to exist within this

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:29.800
 framework and attach itself to museums.

00:32:29.800 --> 00:32:38.790
 And that gives us the freedom to arrange these things as if they are compositions rather than,

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:43.770
 you know, a functioning business or, you know, I think because it's art, it can happen one

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:45.610
 time and never happen again.

00:32:46.350 --> 00:33:00.570
 Um, and I think that's, that's kind of important, but, um, uh, as far as I understand the only hard

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:06.280
 and fast thing that separates this from not art is just that it happens in an art space and we are

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:14.520
 artists and, and, you know, we do really think about how all of these things fit conceptually

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:19.050
 together, what kind of, um, phenomenon they create.

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:25.770
 And, and I think, you know, as much as we're driven to share our research, we're also driven

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:28.210
 to share our wonder and our curiosity.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:33.970
 And I think if it didn't, if that didn't translate, then no, maybe it wouldn't be art.

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:41.980
 You know, maybe it would just be a temporary school for one day or, um, or a way of transplanting

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:43.140
 a farm into the city.

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:52.200
 But I think that chance for wonder or for transformation really keeps it from being an only didactic tool.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:59.840
 I think before we started recording, we were talking about the space of experimentation or the ability

00:33:59.840 --> 00:34:02.900
 to ask questions that don't necessarily have answers.

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:03.460
 Yeah.

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:09.960
 And that comes across really clearly with your description of those events because they're

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:11.820
 not reconciled at the end of it.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:16.230
 There's not, yeah, it's not meant to be succinct in that way.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:23.390
 Well, I think there's a great example in that group of Contra Costa College science and math

00:34:23.390 --> 00:34:25.130
 scholars who came to participate.

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:27.310
 Like they had so much fun.

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:36.230
 They did, they did sort of their role and, um, it was amazing to watch them do it.

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:43.360
 And they were really thoughtful and, and just perfect as collaborators in their role.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:49.900
 And then they all filed out of the museum and went down to Top Dog and had hot dogs for lunch.

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:56.380
 Even though there was all this amazing organic food and, um, you know, the whole place was

00:34:56.380 --> 00:35:03.160
 permeated with food, but they really were like, let's get out of here at least for five minutes.

00:35:03.380 --> 00:35:11.220
 And I think that's like kind of a perfect kind of failure, you know, and, and it points to

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.960
 the experimental nature of what we do.

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:16.280
 Like we're not going to win everything.

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:19.840
 We're just going to mess things up a little bit.

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:26.010
 And, you know, some of those things will land a little bit more toward what we're thinking.

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:34.440
 And some of them will land somewhere else, but, um, but you know, it's, we're more about

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:36.980
 asking questions than about what the answers are.

00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:45.330
 And so maybe with that in mind, what, like, where has there been friction or what has felt

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:48.040
 sticky in exploring these things?

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:53.850
 What are the, what are the top dog lunches of these projects?

00:35:53.950 --> 00:36:00.370
 Be there maybe like in kind of more metaphorical ways or, or in physical ways?

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:08.950
 Well, I think if we're being really honest, there definitely have been moments where like two

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:16.590
 of us find ourselves shoveling a half ton of gravel while nobody else is, you know, you can't

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:17.930
 get anyone else on the phone.

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:24.460
 And that's when you think like, wasn't, wait, weren't we all collaborating?

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:26.120
 Wasn't this fun before?

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:33.440
 But I think, you know, this is, this is group dynamics that you have times when your group

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:36.320
 works well together and you have times when it really doesn't.

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:45.320
 Um, but I think, you know, right now we're in a time of real friction after working together

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.930
 for quite a few years.

00:36:48.390 --> 00:37:03.520
 Um, I, all of us have really full-time lives and the, the joy we feel in collaborating is

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:06.700
 really tempered by all these other considerations.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:09.580
 Like how is Sam's business?

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:13.930
 How, how is Stacy's bakery doing and her kid?

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:18.850
 And, um, what will it be like when Jerome moves to Japan?

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:26.430
 Like in this critical essay by Christine Olin, then she talks about, you know, like on the

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:30.090
 one hand, you're, you guys are really investigating sustainability and access.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:34.550
 And you talked about this a little bit with open water, but on the other hand, it's in

00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:36.250
 Alameda, it's hard for people to get to.

00:37:36.410 --> 00:37:38.410
 And when they get there, it's expensive and they're hungry.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.530
 And maybe that was a surprise.

00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:41.450
 Maybe it wasn't.

00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:48.630
 What does that felt like to ask questions where they're just big questions?

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:51.670
 Like you can't possibly answer all those things no matter.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:38:00.560
 Well, it does feel like asking, you know, and, um, and it gets hard to collaborate when

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:02.120
 it starts to feel like demanding.

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:11.560
 But, um, I think we're asking something from our systems that they are not usually constructed

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:14.610
 to give, you know, we're asking something.

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:22.330
 From the academic dialogue on food and food systems to have it be accessible to people who

00:38:22.330 --> 00:38:23.630
 are not within academia.

00:38:23.630 --> 00:38:35.560
 We're asking something from, um, the fine food industry to, um, also share its bounty with

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:37.120
 schoolyards, you know?

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:43.920
 And, um, and, and sometimes that's a, that's a pretty smooth thing to do.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:49.340
 And sometimes it looks really messy, you know?

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:58.160
 I mean, even after several years of collaborating with Contra Costa College, it's, it's never

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:00.890
 become a totally comfortable thing to do.

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:06.520
 And, um, and we certainly haven't been over backwards trying to bring in more and more schools.

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:12.460
 Um, although, you know, definitely there are other projects that do that really, really

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:12.820
 well.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:20.200
 Um, but, um, you know, in the same way coming from Chez Panisse and not necessarily the cafe,

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:25.230
 which is a little more accessible within that restaurant, but, um, the downstairs restaurant,

00:39:25.350 --> 00:39:29.620
 which is expensive and is kind of a major event to go to.

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:38.970
 You know, I think it is hard to create a bridge between that and the food we eat every day.

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:41.360
 And that bridge is certainly there.

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:49.820
 The more you work with, um, the kind of beautiful material you find it, uh, in the kitchen at

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:54.620
 Chez Panisse, the more it becomes like what you see every day, or the more you can understand

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:57.800
 it as part of the world we already live in.

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:08.780
 But, um, but I think to create a way for a broad audience to engage with that, it, I

00:40:08.780 --> 00:40:14.670
 think probably seemed easier earlier in the process and has gotten harder as we've gone

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:15.530
 on.

00:40:16.120 --> 00:40:22.150
 Um, but I think taking it in smaller pieces has been really joyful for us.

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:32.370
 And so, um, you know, after doing several really, really big events, it has felt good to

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:35.920
 begin doing things on a smaller scale.

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:45.430
 And I think the group of us went to Sweden in 2012 or 2011 to explore the idea of a residency

00:40:45.430 --> 00:40:50.610
 in, uh, modernist housing development outside of Stockholm.

00:40:51.070 --> 00:40:56.290
 And it happens to be home to over a hundred nationalities, you know, a lot of different

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:59.930
 languages, a lot of different cultural experiences.

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:10.200
 And, um, in that case, you know, the conversation was about a long-term, um, collaboration.

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:16.730
 So, you know, on the one hand, there was an encouragement to think big.

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:23.390
 And I think probably every person's mind was sort of like, how small can we get?

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:29.400
 And, and what we ended up doing was just looking at the residency apartment, which is on the first

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:37.550
 floor of this 11 story building among 11 story buildings and thinking, well, what would happen

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:41.890
 if we just opened the windows and people could come to the windows and talk to us?

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:47.700
 And well, we guess we'd have to have a cafe or some reason for people to do that.

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:55.220
 And, and maybe it could be a cafe where the currency is questions and ideas or, um, you know,

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:56.480
 certain kinds of translations.

00:41:56.480 --> 00:42:04.090
 And so, um, we began looking at the food system around us, but, you know, in physical terms,

00:42:04.250 --> 00:42:08.730
 that project started by taking the cabinet doors off so that people could see what we had in

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:13.470
 our pantry and hopefully talk to us about what they had in theirs.

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:26.370
 And I think that, that was a really wonderful way of, of translating the kind of pantry ideas

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:34.810
 of a place like Chez Panis or even of the Bay areas, food ideas, um, to another place, you

00:42:34.810 --> 00:42:41.040
 know, a place where, you know, someone said, of course, my tomatoes taste like cardboard.

00:42:41.210 --> 00:42:42.290
 We're in Sweden.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:49.850
 It's not a, you know, it's a really amazing agricultural situation.

00:42:49.970 --> 00:42:54.250
 And at the same time, their climate is so different than ours.

00:42:54.370 --> 00:43:00.440
 So a lot is imported, a lot is not native, a lot is not growing year round.

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:09.960
 And so, um, you know, to trade back and forth ideas about canning, freezing, sharing, um, uh,

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:18.760
 whole lamb bought from a farmer pretty close by was a really fun experience and led to three

00:43:18.760 --> 00:43:27.650
 years of really amazing and, and always reinvented ideas of what we could do together.

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:35.070
 But, um, it feels like when you talk about this and you seem the most lit up about it,

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:39.290
 it's when these big ideas come down to an interaction with a person.

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:39.750
 Yeah.

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:44.830
 And as I'm sitting here talking to you, I can kind of see your face, see these people

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:48.620
 as you describe these little anecdotal stories.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:51.380
 So I know you did a lot of that in Sweden.

00:43:52.150 --> 00:43:58.710
 Um, what was that like and what did it feel like to go from asking these big questions to

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:05.360
 getting really into the day to day and starting from a different point, I think.

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:05.800
 Yeah.

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:10.200
 Well, maybe a good way to answer that is with, um, an example.

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:21.680
 Um, but one person we met through a chef who happened to contact us who lived in our neighborhood

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:25.730
 was a farmer from whom he gets his meat.

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:31.680
 And she lives about 40 minutes outside of, um, Vidya, which is the town where we worked.

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:39.250
 And her farming methods are very revolutionary in ways that I'm not going to go into now,

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:45.190
 but, um, suffice it to say, she's someone who is practically vegetarian and makes her living

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:52.850
 as an organic meat farmer on a farm where the animals eat food that's been entirely grown

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:53.410
 on the farm.

00:44:53.630 --> 00:44:58.580
 So this is like an unusual situation almost anywhere.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:07.120
 But, um, we walked away from her farm with a series of books on biodynamic farming and organic

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:07.640
 farming.

00:45:07.780 --> 00:45:15.810
 And the next day in the cafe, someone wandered in from two towns over who was, um, a woman

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:23.040
 who was in Sweden had immigrated with her husband from Kenya and, um, she had had a stroke and

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:29.090
 found herself kind of alienated from her surroundings, but she just came and stayed.

00:45:29.650 --> 00:45:39.500
 And, um, um, she told us eventually that she was an agronomer and, um, she took the books

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:42.020
 off the shelf and started really studying them.

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:43.840
 And she took them home with her.

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:51.300
 And, um, we learned from the farmer a little bit later that, um, on her farm, she was hoping

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:57.280
 to create a re-entry program for people who had experienced significant setbacks and who

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:59.450
 were re-entering working life.

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:05.630
 And it looks like Jane, the agronomer is going to be one of the first people who joins that

00:46:05.630 --> 00:46:06.270
 program.

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:14.860
 Um, but I think connections like that are really exciting and they come from kind of being around,

00:46:15.530 --> 00:46:18.170
 uh, being open, being willing to talk.

00:46:18.170 --> 00:46:24.380
 And I think, you know, this is another thing that doesn't exactly make art practice, but

00:46:24.380 --> 00:46:30.860
 I think in Sweden where you don't always see neighbors talking to each other or knocking

00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:38.020
 on each other's doors just to say hi, um, there's something really special about putting yourself

00:46:38.020 --> 00:46:52.220
 in that, uh, unexpected in some ways kind of unbelievable role of, um, soliciting conversation

00:46:52.220 --> 00:46:54.340
 and then showing up for it.

00:46:54.340 --> 00:47:01.450
 Um, so I think there's a kind of juxtaposition there between the expected and then what's

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:04.710
 possible or what could be possible that's important.

00:47:06.950 --> 00:47:11.900
 I feel really struck by, um, using food as a place that everyone's an expert.

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:14.380
 Everybody has something to say.

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:15.840
 Everybody has an opinion.

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:16.770
 Yeah.

00:47:17.950 --> 00:47:19.970
 In that way, it's not like art at all.

00:47:20.340 --> 00:47:20.770
 Yeah.

00:47:20.870 --> 00:47:22.670
 Everybody knows a lot about food.

00:47:23.120 --> 00:47:26.580
 But you know, everyone knows a lot about art too.

00:47:26.580 --> 00:47:33.850
 And I think my most recent project is really like seeking out expertise about art where

00:47:33.850 --> 00:47:39.690
 it lies, you know, like trying to understand the expertise of the trolley driver who's driving

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:48.280
 tourists back and forth past our city's architecture and art and trying to access, not understand,

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:56.640
 but access the perspective of someone whose job it is to maintain murals as a custodian in

00:47:56.640 --> 00:48:00.060
 the Coi Tower or the Maritime Museum or even the SF MoMA.

00:48:00.680 --> 00:48:07.980
 Um, you know, I, I think in that way, all my projects really are about understanding that

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:16.620
 particular resource of expertise and, and, um, you know, when it's, sometimes it seems necessary

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:22.170
 to frame it in a certain way to really, um, create that access.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:28.000
 But when it's there and you make contact, it's so exciting.

00:48:28.550 --> 00:48:35.880
 You know, it's just, I was with a room full of academics the other day and someone just

00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:39.530
 out of the blue said, learning is fun.

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:43.280
 And it was like, the room went silent.

00:48:44.140 --> 00:48:51.580
 Like, you said that also like, Oh my God, you're totally right.

00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:56.210
 But I think, um, I think there is something so beautiful about that.

00:48:56.310 --> 00:49:01.290
 Like feeling yourself learning something that you didn't expect to learn and, and really

00:49:01.290 --> 00:49:02.050
 taking it in.

00:49:02.050 --> 00:49:06.190
 And I think, you know, maybe that's why I do what I do.

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:10.300
 Well, I think I should stop there.

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:12.100
 That's a great place to stop.

